No framework given, so vanilla JS; swap in your selector, endpoint and render function.

```js
const input = document.querySelector('#search');
let timer, controller;

input.addEventListener('input', () => {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(async () => {
    controller?.abort();
    controller = new AbortController();
    try {
      const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(input.value)}`, {
        signal: controller.signal,
      });
      render(await res.json());
    } catch (e) {
      if (e.name !== 'AbortError') throw e;
    }
  }, 300);
});
```

Each keystroke resets the 300ms timer → one call per typing pause. Debounce alone doesn't stop a slow earlier response landing after a newer one, hence the `AbortController`.

React equivalent: same logic in an effect keyed on the query, cleanup cancels both.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => {
    fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(setResults)
      .catch(e => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') throw e; });
  }, 300);
  return () => { clearTimeout(timer); controller.abort(); };
}, [query]);
```

